Both Sydney and Melbourne have several attractions that let a 7‑year‑old enter for free on December 15 2024, and those attractions are open to the public (no ticket reservation needed). Below is a concise side‑by‑side list of the best free‑entry options in each city, plus a short “what to expect” guide so you can decide which destination fits your family’s style.

---

## 🎯 Free‑entry attractions that welcome a 7‑year‑old on **2024‑12‑15**

| City | Attraction (free for a child) | Why it’s great for a 7‑year‑old | Typical opening hours on 15 Dec 2024* |
|------|-------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| **Sydney** | **Royal Botanic Garden Sydney** | Huge lawns, playgrounds, kid‑friendly guided walks, plenty of space to run. | 07:00 – 19:00 (open daily) |
|  | **Australian National Maritime Museum** (children ≤ 12 free) | Interactive ship decks, submarine “USS Tobruk” tour, hands‑on science exhibits. | 09:30 – 17:30 (closed on some public holidays – check website) |
|  | **Sydney Opera House – Public Plaza & Forecourt** | Iconic backdrop, free street performances, easy photo‑ops; you can join a free family‑friendly “Opera House Walk” (usually runs in the morning). | Open 24 h (plaza) |
|  | **Sydney Harbour Bridge – Pylon Lookout (outside area)** | Free to walk around the base; great views of the harbour. | 24 h (outside) |
| **Melbourne** | **Royal Botanic Gardens Melbourne** | Kids’ Garden with water play, wide walking trails, wildlife spotting (birds, turtles). | 07:00 – 19:00 (open daily) |
|  | **National Gallery of Victoria (NGV)** – General admission | Free entry for all ages; family‑friendly art workshops often run on weekends. | 10:00 – 17:00 (closed on some public holidays – check) |
|  | **Melbourne Museum – Children’s Gallery** (children ≤ 16 free) | Hands‑on science, natural history, and a dedicated “Kids Zone”. | 10:00 – 17:00 (closed on certain holidays) |
|  | **Federation Square (public area)** | Open space with occasional free street performances and interactive installations. | 24 h (public area) |

\*Opening hours are the standard schedule for mid‑December; most of these venues stay open on the 15th, but it’s always worth double‑checking a few days before you travel in case of a special holiday closure or maintenance.

---

## 📍 How the two cities compare for a family of three

| Aspect | **Sydney** | **Melbourne** |
|--------|------------|---------------|
| **Nature & Outdoor fun** | Botanic Garden + harbour walks; easy to combine with a ferry ride to Manly or a beach day at Bondi (both free to reach by public transport). | Botanic Gardens + Yarra River walks; easy tram rides to the Gardens and to the nearby **Royal Park** (free). |
| **Cultural & Museum experiences** | Maritime Museum (free for kids) gives a hands‑on look at ships and sea life. The Opera House plaza is iconic and often hosts free performances. | NGV (free art) and Melbourne Museum (interactive kids’ gallery) give a mix of art, science, and natural history. |
| **Ease of getting around** | Well‑connected trains, ferries, and buses; most free sites are within walking distance of the CBD. | Melbourne’s tram network is legendary – you can hop on a free tram (Zone 1) and reach all three free attractions without buying a ticket. |
| **Typical December weather** | Warm to hot (average 22‑26 °C), occasional humidity – great for outdoor garden visits. | Warm (average 20‑24 °C) and generally a bit drier than Sydney – comfortable for walking and museum trips. |
| **Extra kid‑friendly paid options (if you want to add one)** | **Taronga Zoo** (short ferry ride, tickets ~AU$49 adult, AU$24 child) – great animal experience. | **Scienceworks** (interactive science museum, tickets ~AU$20 adult, AU$15 child) – perfect for curious minds. |

---

## ✅ What to do next

1. **Confirm the free‑entry status** – Visit each attraction’s official website (e.g., *Royal Botanic Garden Sydney*, *Australian National Maritime Museum*, *National Gallery of Victoria*, *Melbourne Museum*) and look for the “Admission” or “Family” section for the date **2024‑12‑15**.  
2. **Plan your daily route** – Both cities allow you to cluster the free sites together:  
   *Sydney*: Botanic Garden → Opera House Plaza → Maritime Museum (all within a 15‑minute walk).  
   *Melbourne*: Botanic Gardens → NGV (across the street) → Melbourne Museum (short tram ride).  
3. **Pack for the weather** – Light layers, sunscreen, and a hat for the midday sun.  
4. **Consider a backup paid activity** – If you have extra time or want a special treat, book a ticket for Taronga Zoo (Sydney) or Scienceworks (Melbourne) in advance; both are family‑friendly and have good child‑discount policies.

---

### Bottom line

- **Both cities give you at least three solid free‑entry attractions on the exact day you’re traveling**, so you won’t need to purchase tickets just to keep the 7‑year‑old entertained.  
- **Sydney** leans more toward iconic harbour scenery and maritime history, while **Melbourne** offers a blend of lush gardens, world‑class art, and interactive museum fun.  

Pick the city whose vibe matches your family’s interests (harbour‑side walks vs. art‑and‑science experiences), and you’ll have a stress‑free, cost‑effective day on December 15 2024. Have a wonderful vacation!

